findings.body:
`generate_github_mirror_url_from_base` used to call `cdn.set_path(github_url)` before serialising the mirror URL. That call is now gone, so the function returns the bare mirror base URL and the requested asset path is lost.

With `TAURI_BUNDLER_TOOLS_GITHUB_MIRROR` set, `create_agent_and_url` returns that base URL, and `download()` GETs the mirror root instead of the asset. Every GitHub-hosted tool (linuxdeploy, WiX, NSIS, the appimage plugins) fails for users on the base-URL mirror.

The old `set_path` call was itself odd, because it passed a full URL into a path setter. That suggests the intended mapping needs a deliberate fix, not just deletion. Either restore the path join in a correct form, or drop the base-URL mirror feature and say so. Either way, add a unit test next to the existing template tests, since nothing covers this function today.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/utils/http_utils.rs" lines="33-40"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The removed `set_path` line was the only place the GitHub path reached the mirror. The template-based function still works, so the regression only affects users of the base-URL env var.

`download_webview2_offline_installer` replaces `download(url)` with a direct `ureq::get(url).call()`. That skips `create_agent_and_url` and `base_ureq_agent()`, so the request uses ureq's default agent with the bundled webpki roots and none of the explicit proxy settings.

This is the exact case the PR is meant to fix: the offline installer is large, and it is fetched from networks that often use a custom CA. On those machines this request will still fail with a certificate error, while the HEAD request on the line above (`webview2_guid_path`) succeeds through `base_ureq_agent()`. The result is a half-working path. The rewrite also drops the `log::info!` that `download()` emits.

The fix is to route this request through the shared helper, as the neighbouring code already does.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/windows/util.rs" lines="65-68"}

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/windows/util.rs" lines="65-66"}
let agent = base_ureq_agent();
let response = agent.get(url).call().map_err(Box::new)?;

When a GitHub mirror is configured, `generate_github_alternative_url` returns `ureq::agent()` as the agent. That default agent uses the bundled webpki roots and never gets `RootCerts::PlatformVerifier` or the explicit proxy setup from `base_ureq_agent()`.

So the mirror path, which is usually the path a corporate network would use, still fails with a custom CA. The feature only applies on the non-mirror branch, which is the one that least needs it.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/utils/http_utils.rs" lines="42-54"}

::callout{type="info"}
This is not caused by the diff. It is a gap the diff could close by reusing the same helper.

`crate_version` changes `if lock.is_some() && crate_lock_packages.is_empty()` to `!crate_lock_packages.is_empty()`. This is not related to certificates, is not mentioned in the PR description or changelog, and changes visible output.

Before this change, the block effectively never set `lock_version`. For zero entries the join produced an empty string, which was then discarded. For several entries the block was skipped entirely.

After this change, `lock_version` is set whenever the lock has multiple entries for the crate. `Display` in the same file renders it as `name version (v1, v2)`, so the CLI's version output changes for those crates. That may be the correct fix, but it should be its own PR with a changelog entry and a test, not hidden in a certificate change.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/helpers/cargo_manifest.rs" lines="194-194"}

::callout{type="warning"}
If the author wants to keep this fix, split it out and add a test covering multi-version lock entries. Otherwise revert it.

The author asked whether this should be on by default or behind a flag. The PR answers by putting `platform-certs` in `default` for both `tauri-bundler` and `tauri-cli`. Every user of these crates now gets a different trust store, plus `rustls-platform-verifier`, its Android companion, and a second `jni` 0.19 in the dependency tree. The only opt-out is disabling default features.

Default-on is reasonable if it is deliberate, since system CA trust is what most users expect. Still, it changes TLS behavior for everyone on upgrade, and the changelog only says the bundler and CLI "will now read TLS Certificates installed on the system" without noting that this is on by default or how to disable it. I'd like that decision stated in the changelog, with the opt-out documented.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-cli/Cargo.toml" lines="141-149"}

::callout{type="info"}
Default-on is the right call for the reported problem, but the changelog should say so.

The only tests in `http_utils.rs` cover `generate_github_mirror_url_from_template`. Nothing exercises `base_ureq_agent`, `generate_github_mirror_url_from_base`, or the `crate_version` change. The mirror regression above would have been caught by one assertion on the URL it returns.

Testing the TLS config directly is harder, but a compile-only check under `cfg(feature = "platform-certs")` would at least catch a feature rename or a missing forward.

::code{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/utils/http_utils.rs" lines="33-40"}

`base_ureq_agent` ends with `return agent;`, which clippy flags as `needless_return`. The `cfg` split also repeats the builder chain in two places.

Separately, `crate_latest_version` builds its own platform-verifier agent inline rather than reusing a helper, and that agent does not set the explicit proxy that `base_ureq_agent` does. tauri-cli cannot import the bundler's `pub(crate)` helper, so the cleanest fix is a shared constructor or at least one place for both builders.

